                  "obligationId": "scenario%3Afile-owner-without-destination-write-grant::entry%3Apost-process-file",
                  "decision": {
                    "kind": "observed",
                    "observed": "allow"
                  },
                  "branchResults": [],
                  "explanation": "At the declared POST /process/file boundary, the source-visible path allows the stated verified non-admin to cause a write to the caller-selected existing collection. Authentication binds user through the verified-user dependency, and the non-admin branch restricts lookup of the source file by both file ID and user ID. That is a source-file ownership/access control only. The caller-supplied collection_name is then selected as the destination and passed to save_docs_to_vector_db with add=True whenever a collection name was supplied. On the supplied existing-collection path, existence is detected, but the no-write short circuit applies only when add is False; the downstream sink inserts items into the selected collection. No destination-knowledge-base ownership or explicit-write-grant check is visible on this supplied path. Thus, under the task premises that the collection already exists and the principal lacks destination ownership or a write grant, source-file ownership remains distinct from destination permission and does not block the destination write. The destination write sink is VECTOR_DB_CLIENT.insert with collection_name and items. This is a source-supported failure relative to the accepted deny policy, expressed by the required observed decision as allow; it is limited to the declared entry and supplied fixed context and makes no repository-wide or deployment claim.",
                  "facts": [
                    {
                      "id": "fact-entry",
                      "kind": "entry",
                      "statement": "The declared entry is POST /process/file, whose handler receives the form, a verified user dependency, and a database session.",
